            <ArticleTitle>Systemic Inflammatory Response Is a Prognostic Marker in HIV-Infected Patients with Hepatocellular Carcinoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000479521</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is increasingly prevalent in people living with HIV. Systemic inflammation is a prognostic factor requiring validation in HIV-associated HCC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Using a multi-centre database of consecutive HCC cases, we investigated the prognostic role of a panel of inflammatory markers, including neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio (NLR), using univariate and multivariate survival analyses.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Fifty-nine patients with HIV-associated HCC secondary to hepatitis C (69%) or B virus infection (32%) were identified. The median survival was 22 months. A raised NLR independently predicted patients' survival and was correlated with advanced Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer stage (p = 0.003) and poor performance status (p &lt; 0.001) but not with HIV RNA or CD4 counts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Systemic inflammation, as measured by NLR, is a prognostic determinant associated with adverse pathological features of malignancy, but not coexisting HIV infection, suggesting a tumour-promoting role of the innate immune response that warrants further investigation in mechanistic studies.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 S. Karger AG, Basel.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
